Dune is optional. For projects that do use it, `sqlgg migrations dune` prints
ordinary Dune rules that wrap `sqlgg migrations generate`; nothing is written
to disk and there is no script to copy around.

  $ cat > dune-project <<'DUNE'
  > (lang dune 3.0)
  > DUNE
  $ cat > schema.sql <<'SQL'
  > CREATE TABLE users (id INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);
  > SQL
  $ : > migrations.sql
  $ sqlgg migrations dune --dialect mysql --lang ocaml --schema schema.sql --migrations migrations.sql --generated migrations.ml --module Migrations --now 20261112000000 > sqlgg_migrations.inc
  $ cat sqlgg_migrations.inc
  ; Generated by `sqlgg migrations dune`.
  (rule
   (targets "migrations.ml" "migrations.sql.next")
   (deps "schema.sql" "migrations.sql")
   (action
    (progn
     (with-stdout-to "migrations.sql.next"
      (cat "migrations.sql"))
     (with-stdout-to "migrations.ml"
      (run %{bin:sqlgg} "migrations" "generate" "--dialect" "mysql" "--lang" "ocaml" "--schema" "schema.sql" "--migrations" "migrations.sql.next" "--module" "Migrations" "--now" "20261112000000")))))
  
  (rule
   (alias sqlgg-migrations)
   (action
    (diff "migrations.sql" "migrations.sql.next")))
  
  (alias
   (name check)
   (deps
    (alias sqlgg-migrations)))
  $ cat > dune <<'DUNE'
  > (include sqlgg_migrations.inc)
  > DUNE

The schema has a table the migration log does not know about. The build
regenerates the module from a copy of the log and reports what the committed
log is missing:

  $ dune build @sqlgg-migrations 2>&1 | grep -v '^\(---\|+++\|index\|diff\) '
  appended 1 migration(s) to migrations.sql.next (id 20261112000000_create_users)
  File "migrations.sql", line 1, characters 0-0:
  @@ -0,0 +1,4 @@
  +-- [sqlgg] generated
  +-- [sqlgg] id=20261112000000_create_users
  +CREATE TABLE `users` (`id` INT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (`id`));
  +DROP TABLE `users`;
  $ grep -c make_generated_reversible _build/default/migrations.ml
  1

Promoting accepts the new migration into the committed log, after which the
build is quiet. `@check` runs the same comparison:

  $ dune promote
  Promoting _build/default/migrations.sql.next to migrations.sql.
  $ cat migrations.sql
  -- [sqlgg] generated
  -- [sqlgg] id=20261112000000_create_users
  CREATE TABLE `users` (`id` INT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (`id`));
  DROP TABLE `users`;
  $ dune build @sqlgg-migrations @check 2>&1 | grep -v 'nothing new to migrate'
  [1]

A later schema change goes through the same loop:

  $ cat > schema.sql <<'SQL'
  > CREATE TABLE users (id INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL);
  > SQL
  $ dune build @check 2>&1 | grep '^+[^+]'
  +-- [sqlgg] generated
  +-- [sqlgg] id=20261112000001_alter_users_add_col_name
  +ALTER TABLE `users` ADD COLUMN `name` TEXT NOT NULL;
  +ALTER TABLE `users` DROP COLUMN `name`;
  $ dune promote
  Promoting _build/default/migrations.sql.next to migrations.sql.
  $ dune build @check 2>&1 | grep -v 'nothing new to migrate'
  [1]
  $ grep -c make_generated_reversible _build/default/migrations.ml
  2

File names are always written as quoted Dune strings:

  $ sqlgg migrations dune --dialect tidb --lang ocaml --schema 'my schema.sql' --migrations 'log "v2".sql' --manual 'by hand.sql' --generated migrations.ml --module Migrations | grep -e '(deps "' -e 'run %'
   (deps "my schema.sql" "log \"v2\".sql" "by hand.sql")
      (run %{bin:sqlgg} "migrations" "generate" "--dialect" "tidb" "--lang" "ocaml" "--schema" "my schema.sql" "--migrations" "log \"v2\".sql.next" "--module" "Migrations" "--manual" "by hand.sql")))))
